  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 115 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nThe digging trough that's currently over the west coast will\nmerge with the remnants of Hurricane Polo and bring us some much\nneeded rain to end the week. Timing for the rain has once again\nbeen pushed back. We now don't have rain entering most of the \narea until Thursday afternoon. There are a couple of general\nthunder outlooks from SPC for the next couple of days. The \nstrongest part of the trough will move through Thursday \nafternoon and could bring us a few rumbles of thunder and maybe\na few isolated thunderstorms. Moisture and lift and low-level\nbulk shear (~30 kts) will be there, but instability is in \nquestion as MUCAPE values are ~500 J/kg. The trough over the \narea will be impacted by another trough moving through the \nnorthern Plains bringing us a cold front for the weekend. A \nnortherly wind shift will occur during the day Friday, but the \ndewpoint drop won't occur until Sunday until a secondary front \nmoves through. The first front could sweep through rather \nquickly and clear out the rain by Saturday morning, or it \nsticks around and stalls out over southern MO bringing the area \nmore rain until that secondary front moves through on Sunday. \nRight now, we are going with the stalled front scenario with \nrain continuing through Saturday morning (30-50% chance of \nrain) and ending north to south Saturday evening.\n\nFlooding: There is a flooding threat mainly west of Highway 65\nwhere the higher axis of forecasted rainfall totals are \nsituated. Though, rainfall totals continue to decline as they\nhave been over the past few forecast cycles. The trough will \nmove into KS/OK and take its time moving east. It seems to \nbecome elongated and string out over the Central Plains. This \nsystem will be tropical-like and bring an abundance of moisture \nwith it. Model PWATs with this system are record- breaking with \nvalues for this time of year near 2\". Our daily max right now is\n1.49\", so this clearly will be a moist airmass moving in. \nExpect the rainfall to occur in bands. This also means there may\nbe sharp cut offs in rainfall amounts if you are located just \noutside of or too far to the east of these bands. We are in a D2\nto D3 drought, which indicates that our soils could use the \nmoisture. Even with that information, we still may issue a Flood\nWatch for counties at least west of I-49 due to the possibility\nof training heavy bands of rain for 24-48 hours. Showers over \nin that area will be efficient rainfall producers and will \nlikely have high rain rates. Areas in drought could still see \nflash flooding from this activity if the rain rates are high \nenough. \n\nTemperatures: The rain will take its time spreading west to \neast on Thursday which will give us a 10-15 degree spread in \ntemperatures across the area. Where the clouds and rain are, \nhighs will stay in the lower 70s. Out over the eastern Ozarks \nwhere most of Thursday will be dry for them, they will be in the\nmid to upper 80s. By Friday, everyone will experience the \ncooldown with highs in the 70s. Those highs stick around through\nthe weekend and into the start of next week with lows in the \n50s.",
